  "description": "Two 35-year-olds two years out of training. Household gross ~$410k (physician ~$325k + spouse ~$85k), ~$270,000 net. $150,000 invested. Real income held flat. Isolates how much of the retirement-versus-working consumption gap is explained by healthcare costs the main grid omits. Savings rate is crossed with three healthcare assumptions; every other decision is held at retire 65, standard loans, typical house, two children in public school. Medicare figures follow Fidelity 2026 ($185,500 per individual over retirement, excluding long-term care), modeled as $8,000/yr at 65 growing 2.5%/yr real. The long-term-care line is a rough expected-value allowance, not a sourced estimate. Real 2026 dollars, net of tax.",
